A range loop generates numeric values directly from its loop header.

[codeblock, $code("moth"):
    loop 0 to 10 by 2 |value, index|:
        io.line([: [index]: [value]])
    ;
]

### Forms of range loops

[codeblock, $code("moth"):
    loop start to end:
        ...

    loop start to & end |value|:
        ...

    loop start to end by step |value, index|:
        ...

    loop to end |value|:
        ...

    loop to & end |value|:
        ...
]

### Bounds

- `to` uses an exclusive end bound.
- `to &` uses an inclusive end bound.
- `loop to end` starts at `0`.
- `loop to & end` starts at `0` and includes the end value.
- Ascending bounds count upward.
- Descending bounds count downward.
- There is no `reverse` keyword.
- When start and end are equal, an exclusive range has no iterations and an inclusive range has one.

Range operands are exact fixed-width integers (`I8` through `U64`) or binary
floats (`F16`, `F32` and `F64`). `Byte`, `Dec` and `DecN` have no range domain.
Mixed operands follow the exact numeric domain rules in @../numbers (Numbers).
The selected range type is the counter domain, not a positional index type.

- Without `by`, fixed-integer ranges use a step magnitude of `1`. Omitted zero and default one values materialise in the selected range type.
- `by` supplies a magnitude. The compiler applies the direction implied by the bounds.
- A literal zero step is rejected during compilation.
- A non-literal step is checked before the first iteration. A value of zero fails at runtime.
- Any range using a binary float requires an explicit `by` step.
- Start, end and explicit step expressions must have numeric types.

[codeblock, $code("moth"):
    loop 0.0 to 1.0 by 0.1 |amount|:
        io.line([: [amount]])
    ;
]

### Range loop evaluation order

Start, end and explicit step expressions are evaluated once, from left to
right, before the first bounds check.

The loop keeps those evaluated values for the complete iteration.

### Bindings

The first binding receives the current range value in the selected exact counter
type. Mixed bound and step types follow the numeric domain rules above. Range
counters keep their selected domain rather than inheriting a minimum width.

The optional second binding receives the zero-based iteration position and
always has type `I32`. The counter domain and position type remain independent.
Collection lengths, collection indices and fixed capacities also use `I32`.

Bindings use the same one-or-two-name `|...|` rules as collection loops.

### Checked updates

Runtime range counter updates use checked numeric operations. Their failures
are implicit built-in failure, delivered by the enclosing handler or function
contract. Entry-selected `start` has built-in `Error!`. See @../errors (Errors)
for propagation and recovery, and @../progress (Progress) for current support.

`by` stays a magnitude in every domain. A descending unsigned range subtracts
that magnitude with checked arithmetic, because unary minus is invalid on every
`U*` type.

Termination happens at the last valid endpoint. The loop never needs a counter
value outside the selected domain, so an inclusive range ending at a type's
maximum and a descending range reaching zero both complete normally. An
inclusive `U64` maximum and a descending `U64` range reaching zero terminate
without an invalid successor.

Dynamic zero steps follow the existing failure contract: a literal zero step is
rejected during compilation, while a non-literal zero step fails before the
first iteration.

Floating-point ranges round each candidate to the selected binary precision before
checking the endpoint. A non-finite or out-of-range candidate ends the loop
without becoming the next value. If an in-range candidate rounds back to the
current value before reaching the endpoint, the loop fails at runtime rather
than repeating that value. An inclusive range exits after visiting its
endpoint without computing another candidate.

Const range loops diagnose statically known counter failures while folding.

Fixed-width range domains are accepted end-state design; @../progress (Progress)
records current implementation and backend support.
